.TH OLLAMUX 1 "2026-11-24" "ollamux 0.9.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ollamux \- key-rotating reverse proxy for the Ollama Cloud API
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ollamux
.RB [ \-\-addr
.IR HOST:PORT ]
.RB [ \-\-keys
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-usage-aware[= PCT ]]
.RB [ \-\-stats-file
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-no-stats ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.br
.B ollamux
.B \-\-help
.br
.B ollamux
.B \-\-version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ollamux
gives several Ollama Cloud API keys and serves them combined behind one
local endpoint. Requests are spread across keys (round-robin,
least-loaded first), and when a key is rate-limited, rejected, or
invalid, the request silently retries on the next key before anything
reaches the client.
.PP
This is not a local Ollama server: it serves no models, it proxies to
.I https://ollama.com\fR.
Point Ollama clients at it via
.B OLLAMA_HOST=http://localhost:11435\fR,
or OpenAI-compatible clients via base URL
.IR http://localhost:11435/v1 .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-addr " " \fIHOST:PORT\fR
Listen address. Default:
.IR 127.0.0.1:11435 .
The default port deliberately differs from Ollama's 11434 so both can
run side by side.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keys " " \fIPATH\fR
Keys file. Default, in order of precedence: the
.B OLLAMUX_KEYS
environment variable (newline- or comma-separated keys), then
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/ollamux/keys\fR,
then
.IR ~/.config/ollamux/keys .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Verbose stderr logging: upstream error snippets and cooldown
transitions. Without it, one summary line per request is emitted.
.TP
.B \-\-usage-aware[= \fIPCT \fR]
Quota-aware key selection: keys whose latest known session usage is
at/over
.I PCT
percent are served last (never excluded). Default threshold: 80.
Also settable via
.BR OLLAMUX_USAGE_AWARE ;
the flag wins when both are given. An invalid threshold is a usage
error (exit 2). The usage data is polled every 60 seconds.
.TP
.BR \-\-stats-file " " \fIPATH\fR
Where local request/token counters are persisted. Default, in order:
.B $OLLAMUX_STATS_FILE\fR, then
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME/ollamux/stats.json\fR, then
.IR ~/.local/state/ollamux/stats.json ,
else
.IR ./ollamux-stats.json .
The file is written atomically every 5 seconds and after the shutdown
drain; requests still running when the drain deadline expires are
force-closed and cannot be recorded.
.TP
.B \-\-no-stats
Genuinely disable local counting and persistence (also
.BR OLLAMUX_NO_STATS=1 ;
explicit flags win). /_stats then reports enabled:false and retains no
key, model or endpoint names.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.SH KEYS FILE
One API key per line, in the form
.BR KEY[:N]@ACCOUNT
(get keys at
.IR https://ollama.com/settings/keys ).
The
.B @ACCOUNT
label is required: balance and usage are account-scoped upstream, and
keys sharing a label are treated as one ollama.com account (for example
a group of friends pooling separate accounts). Labels are 1\(en64
characters of
.BR A-Z a-z 0-9 " . _ -" .
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored. Optionally append
.B :N
to a key to set that key's concurrency limit \- the number of cloud
models the account may run at once (free tier 1, pro 3, max 10; the
default when no suffix is given is 3):
.PP
.RS
.nf
your-free-key...:1@free-acct
your-pro-key...:3@pro-acct
your-max-key...:10@max-acct
.fi
.RE
.PP
Requests beyond a key's concurrency limit queue briefly rather than
hammering upstream; waiters beyond that get an honest 429. Duplicate
key entries, missing or invalid account labels, and invalid concurrency
counts are startup errors, not warnings. Slots are counted per key while
the upstream plan limit is per account, so several keys under one label
multiply the in-flight bound (ollamux warns at startup). The file should
be readable only by the user running ollamux:
.B chmod 600 ~/.config/ollamux/keys\fR.
.SH ENDPOINTS
.TS
tab(|);
l l.
/api/*|Proxied to https://ollama.com with key rotation
/v1/*|Proxied (OpenAI-compatible surface) with rotation
/_keys|Per-key health JSON (key suffixes and account labels only, no secrets; tier + embeds latest known usage when a usage snapshot exists)
/_usage|Per-account and per-key usage JSON + pool aggregate (balance, one fetch per account, 60s cache); ?range=24h,7d,30d adds per-account model/time stats; ?refresh=1 forces (one attempt per 5s)
/_stats|Local per-request counters: requests, upstream-reported tokens, duration and retries by key/account/model/endpoint, in UTC hour/day buckets; ?range=24h,7d,30d (default 24h)
/_health|Liveness JSON: {"ok":...,"keys":...,"total_slots":...}
.TE
.PP
Any other path answers 404 with a hint.
.SH USAGE INTROSPECTION
Ollama Cloud publishes plan usage on the documented
.B GET /api/balance
upstream endpoint (docs.ollama.com/api/balance), one request per
.I account
(keys sharing an
.B @ACCOUNT
label), rate-limited to 10 requests per minute per user, shared across
that account's keys.
ollamux decodes it tolerantly: payload drift is reported as a per-key
error in
.BR /_usage ,
never a crash. Legacy plans (session/weekly windows) report what
remains of each window's allowance, reset instants included; ollamux
inverts that to the used fraction of the plan's cap (0.0\(en1.0), not a
token count, with one-decimal percent mirrors (session_pct,
weekly_pct) and session_resets_at/weekly_resets_at for countdowns.
Credit plans answer with USD amounts instead (included_usd,
allowance_usd, purchased_usd) and carry no window fractions.
Transient failures (429, 5xx, network blips) carry the key's last
good row, stamped
.I as_of
with its original fetch time; persistent ones (unauthorized, endpoint
gone, payload drift) surface as per-key error strings. A 429'd key
also sits out the next round \(em or the server's
.B Retry-After
capped at 5 minutes \(em so large pools rotate the fetch budget
across keys instead of losing data. Proxied
.B /api/usage
requests are not special: they go through normal key rotation and
reflect whichever key served them. Usage checks never touch key
health: a 401 there is reported in /_usage but does not mark the key
dead. /_keys is a pure in-memory read and never triggers upstream
usage calls. Each key's plan tier is inferred from its per-key
concurrency (free=1, pro=3, max=10; more concurrency than normal is
the next tier up), and /_usage also reports the pool-wide
capacity-weighted mean of every reporting
.I account's
fraction weighted by its tier's cap (free \(mu1, pro \(mu50, max
\(mu250) \(em a fraction in 0.0\(en1.0 like the per-key numbers, so a
single-account pool reproduces that account's own fraction \(em keys
sharing one account contribute once, never double-counted. Accounts on
cooldown and accounts whose latest fetch failed transiently still
contribute (carried rows keep their last known good fraction); an
unauthorized account is expected to fail its own usage fetch the same
way and contributes nothing (its weight is excluded from the
denominator). Windows nobody reported are null, never 0, and when every
reporting account of a window carries the same resets_at the aggregate
publishes that instant (session_resets_at, weekly_resets_at) so
consumers get one honest reset instant for the pool. Credit-plan rows
sit out the aggregate (their unit is USD, not a fraction of an
allowance).
.PP
With
.B ?range=24h
,
.B ?range=7d
or
.BR ?range=30d ,
/_usage additionally reads the documented
.B GET /api/usage
endpoint once per account and adds a
.B models
section: per-account request counts, tokens and USD cost in hourly
(24h) or daily (7d, 30d) UTC buckets, broken down by model, plus a
pool total summed per account. Up to 10 models appear per
.B groups
array; the rest are combined under
.BR remainder .
The endpoint has no per-request log, and custom ranges plus per-API-key
or per-member breakdowns are not yet published upstream. Members of one
Ollama team share an account and cannot be separated.
.SH LOCAL TOKEN STATISTICS
.B GET /_stats
answers with counters measured from traffic through ollamux (unlike
.BR /_usage ,
which mirrors account-scoped numbers from ollama.com): request counts,
input/cached/output tokens, wall duration, retries and final status,
aggregated per key (account label + fingerprint), account, model, endpoint,
HTTP status, and UTC hour/day buckets. The optional query
.B ?range=24h|7d|30d
selects the bucket series (default 24h: the current partial hour plus
24 previous, i.e. 25 hourly buckets; 7d/30d are daily).
.PP
Token counts are read from the upstream response body
(prompt_eval_count/eval_count/prompt_eval_cached_count on the native API;
the OpenAI-compatible usage object on /v1/*, including Responses
input_tokens/output_tokens). No counts are synthesized; a successful (2xx)
response on a token-bearing endpoint that carried no counters increments
requests_without_usage and contributes zero tokens. Streaming NDJSON and
SSE are sniffed incrementally with bounded per-chunk work. Management
endpoints such as /api/show, /api/ps and /api/embeddings are not counted
as missing usage.
.PP
Only the bucket series and window_totals are range-scoped; totals and all
dimension rows are lifetime (totals_scope:"lifetime"). Key identity is a
stable non-secret fingerprint in addition to the four-character display
suffix.
.PP
Counters persist across restarts (see
.BR \-\-stats-file ).
Retention is bounded to 25 hourly and 31 daily buckets, 512 historical
key rows, 256 accounts, 256 models and 128 endpoints (overflow folds into
.BR <other> );
there is no per-request log and no prompt or completion content is
stored. A file is adopted only when it carries the ollamux-stats marker
and the current schema version; corrupt, foreign, non-regular,
larger-than-16-MiB or newer-versioned files (and symlinked paths, which
are treated as non-regular) are left untouched and this process runs in
memory instead of overwriting them
(shown as persistence:"blocked" in /_stats). Write failures are logged
and reported as last_flush_error. Flushes use unique temp files and an
advisory unix owner lock, so a second instance is blocked rather than
tearing the file. Write failures set last_flush_error and
persistence:"error" until a successful flush. Configured keys with no
traffic yet appear with zero counters, and historical rows from a previous keys file are marked
present:false. With --no-stats, /_stats reports enabled:false and
retains no counters or names.
.SH QUOTA-AWARE KEY SELECTION
With
.B \-\-usage-aware[= \fIPCT \fR]
(see OPTIONS), ollamux polls the balance endpoint every 60 seconds and
demotes keys whose session usage is at/over the threshold behind fresher
keys during candidate selection \(em demoted, never excluded: an
over-quota key still serves when no fresh key has a free slot. The
session window alone drives demotion; credit-plan keys are exempt
(USD amounts carry no session percent to compare) and are ordered
like unmeasured keys. Failed refreshes keep the previous snapshot.
.SH FAILOVER BEHAVIOR
Rate-limited keys (429) cool down for 60 seconds, or the server's
.B Retry-After
value capped at 5 minutes; the request retries on the next key.
Invalid keys (401/403) are marked dead until restart. Upstream 5xx and
network errors fail over to the next key; three consecutive failures
put a key in cooldown. Everything else (e.g. a 400 from a malformed
request) is passed through untouched. Failover happens before the first
response byte, so streaming responses (NDJSON and SSE) are never
corrupted mid-flight.
.PP
Response headers identify the serving key
.B X-Ollamux-Key
(suffix only) and the retry count
.BR X-Ollamux-Retries .
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGINT
(ctrl-c) or
.B SIGTERM
starts a graceful drain for up to 5 seconds before exit; workers keep
serving queued and in-flight requests until the deadline (an instance
that stays idle for about 0.3 seconds exits early), then anything still
running is force-closed. A second signal force-quits immediately (exit
code 130).
.SH SECURITY
The proxy has no authentication: anything that can reach the listen
address can use your API quota. The default binds the loopback
interface only; do not expose it further. Keep the keys file
owner-readable (0600); ollamux warns at startup if it is group- or
world-readable.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ollamux/keys
Default keys file (honours
.BR XDG_CONFIG_HOME ).
.TP
.I /etc/ollamux/keys
Conventional location for system-wide deployments (see
.BR systemd.unit (5)
and the shipped
.IR ollamux.service ).
.TP
.I $OLLAMUX_STATS_FILE
Local token/request statistics, when set.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/ollamux/stats.json
Default statistics location (else
.IR ~/.local/state/ollamux/stats.json ,
else
.IR ./ollamux-stats.json ).
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/ollamux/stats.json.lock
Advisory cross-process owner lock for the statistics file.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OLLAMUX_KEYS
API keys, newline- or comma-separated; overrides the keys file unless
.B \-\-keys
is given.
.TP
.B OLLAMUX_USAGE_AWARE
Enables quota-aware routing with the given percent threshold (1\(en99);
equivalent to
.BR \-\-usage-aware= \fIPCT \fR.
The command-line flag wins when both are set.
.TP
.B OLLAMUX_STATS_FILE
Overrides the statistics-file location (see
.BR \-\-stats-file ).
.TP
.B OLLAMUX_NO_STATS
Disables local counting and persistence; explicit stats flags win.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
Base directory for the default keys location.
.TP
.B XDG_STATE_HOME
Base directory for the default statistics location.
.TP
.B OLLAMA_HOST
Not read by ollamux; exported by clients to point them at the proxy
(example:
.BR http://localhost:11435 ).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean shutdown after drain.
.TP
.B 2
Command-line usage error.
.TP
.B 1
Startup failure: unparseable or missing keys, unbindable address.
.TP
.B 130
Interrupted: second termination signal during drain.
